  "text": "深入求解过程\n Let \\( q(x) \\) be a cubic polynomial such that \\((q(x))^2 - x^2\\) is divisible by \\((x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 9)\\).\n\nFirst, we know that if \\((x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 9)\\) divides \\((q(x))^2 - x^2\\), then the roots 2, -2, and 9 must satisfy \\((q(x))^2 - x^2 = 0\\).\n\nSo we can write:\n\\[ (q(x))^2 - x^2 = [(q(x) - x)(q(x) + x)] = 0 \\]\nThus, we need:\n\\[ q(x) - x = 0 \\] or \\[ q(x) + x = 0 \\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[ q(x) = x \\quad \\text{or} \\quad q(x) = -x \\]\n\nNext, we will test both possible solutions \\( q(x) = x \\) and \\( q(x) = -x \\) to find out which one fits the requirement.\n\nIf \\( q(x) = x \\), then:\n\\[ (q(x))^2 - x^2 = (x)^2 - x^2 = 0 \\]\nThis is divisible by \\((x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 9)\\), so \\( q(x) = x \\) is a valid possibility.\n\nIf \\( q(x) = -x \\), then:\n\\[ (q(x))^2 - x^2 = (-x)^2 - x^2 = x^2 - x^2 = 0 \\]\nThis is also divisible by \\((x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 9)\\), so \\( q(x) = -x \\) is another possible solution.\n\nWe need to check if either solution fits the requirement correctly by comparing it with \\((x - 2)(x + 2)(x - 9) = x^3 - 5x^2 + 6x - 36\\). Based on this, we see that:\n\n\\[ q(x) = x \\]\nFinding \\( q(14) \\):\n\\[ q(14) = 14 \\]\n\nHowever, another solution has been proposed:\n\\[ q(x) = -x \\]\nAgain, finding \\( q(14) \\):\n\\[ q(14) = -14 \\]\n\nSo we need a final answer to stick to one of these values. Given the process and logic, we will choose the one that fits firmly in polynomial terms.\n\nSo the final average of both proposed solutions will be:\n\\[ q(14) = 14 \\]",
